1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. When something breaks in a clawed integration the reflex is to jump straight to a

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
retry. That's often the wrong move because you can't choose a recovery for an error that you

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
haven't classified or fully understood. This lesson covers how to name the failure first

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
by type and by origin. Let's jump in. Let's anchor three terms the whole lesson leans on.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, error type identification. That's the diagnostic step of classifying what kind of

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
failure you're looking at before you touch a fix. It comes first and it decides the recovery.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Second, a transient error. That's a timeout, a rate limit, or a server-side 500 series error,

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
the kind of failure that's expected to clear on its own. So retrying is reasonable. Third,

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
a permanent error. That's a bad request or bad credentials, something that won't clear

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
no matter how many times you retry. It needs a fix, a fallback, or escalation. Identify first,

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
then act. Here's the split that matters most. A transient error is timeouts, rate limits,

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
network blips, and 500 series server errors. In each case the request was well formed,

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
the system was just briefly unavailable, so the very same call can succeed a moment later.

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
A permanent error is different. A 400, a 401, a 403, a 404, or a 413 all point at the request

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
itself. Something about what you sent is wrong, so repeating it unchanged only reproduces the

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
same failure. The tell is simple. Ask whether waiting and trying again could plausibly help.

17
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
If yes, it's transient. If no, you have to change something first.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Transient errors tend to announce themselves through a few familiar signals. A 429 rate limit

19
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
error means you've sent more requests than your limit allows for the moment. A 500 API error or

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
a 504 timeout error is the server telling you something went wrong or took too long on its side.

21
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And a 529 overloaded error shows up when the platform is briefly saturated across all users.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
What these share is that none of them blame your payload. The request was fine, the timing was bad,

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
so the right instinct is patience, a short wait, and another attempt,

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
rather than tearing your request apart looking for a mistake that isn't there.

25
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Permanent errors are the mirror image. They live in the request, so retrying changes nothing.

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A 400 error means the payload is malformed, maybe a missing field or a bad value. A 401

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
authentication error means the key is wrong, revoked, or expired. A 413 request too large

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
means you've sent more than the endpoint accepts, and the messages API caps a single request

29
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
at 32 megabytes. In every case, the fix is to change something and try the corrected call,

30
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
not to fire the same broken one again. Retrying a permanent error just delays the moment you

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
admit the request needs work. When the API returns an error, the body has a predictable shape,

32
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
and three fields carry the weight. First is type, a stable machine-readable label like authentication

33
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:26,000
error or overloaded error. That's the field your code should branch on. Second is message, a human

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
readable sentence describing the problem. It's great for your logs, but don't write logic that

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
parses its wording, because that text can change. Third is request ID, an identifier that's also

36
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
echoed in the response header. Capture it every time, because when you open a support ticket,

37
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
that one string lets the team find your exact request. Branch on type, log the message,

38
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,000
keep the request ID. Now the second lens, origin. A model output error lives in what

39
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Claude actually generated, a wrong plan, a bad tool argument, or a truncated answer. The important

40
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
part is that this rides inside a successful 200 response. The call worked, the generation is the

41
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
problem. An integration error is the opposite. It lives in your surrounding code, the request you

42
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:24,000
built, the way you force the reply, your auth, or the endpoint, and it usually surfaces as an HTTP

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
status. One wrinkle worth knowing. When you stream a response, an error can arrive after a 200 as an

44
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
event in the stream, so a transport failure can hide behind a success code if you only check the

45
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
status. The hardest failures make no noise at all. You get a 200, no exception fires, and the content

46
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
is quietly wrong. Maybe the answer is incomplete or malformed. Maybe the stop reason came back as

47
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
max tokens, which means the output was cut off part way. Maybe the response parses cleanly against

48
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
your schema but carries the wrong values. Every one of these sails straight past a try accept block

49
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
and a status code check because nothing technically errored. The only thing that catches a silent

50
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:17,000
failure is validating the output itself, checking it for completeness, plausibility, and correct

51
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
structure before anything downstream trusts it. Assume success isn't the same as correct.

52
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
One last distinction because these look identical from the outside. A refusal and a bug. A refusal

53
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,000
is claud intentionally declining on policy or safety grounds. That's valid behavior, not a

54
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
malfunction, so the right move is to read stop details for the reason, and if the request is

55
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
legitimate, adjust how you're asking. A bug is an unintended failure, either in your integration

56
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
code or in the model output, and it needs a real fix. Both leave you without a useful result, which

57
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
is why they get confused. Escalating a refusal as a system fault or waving off a real bug

58
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
as an expected decline both send you down the wrong path. Let's recap. Classifying the failure

59
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:13,000
is its own step, and it turns on whether it clears on retry and where it came from. Transient clears,

60
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:21,000
permanent needs a change, model output rides a 200, and integration shows a status. Just remember,

61
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:28,000
a silent failure and a refusal can both masquerade as no answer. That covers classifying

62
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
errors by type and origin. I'll see you in the next one.

